what is GRC

GRC = Governance, Risk & Compliance. It's the function that makes sure the company operates within its rules, manages risk deliberately, and can prove it to auditors and regulators.

Governance: who decides what. Policies, standards, approval paths, ownership. Example: "Any new AI use case must be approved by the AI review board."
Risk: identifying, rating, and handling risks: mitigate, accept, transfer, or avoid. Example: rating a chatbot as "high risk" because it touches customer financial data.
Compliance: meeting external requirements (laws, regulators, frameworks) and internal ones, backed by evidence. Example: showing auditors logs proving AI access controls work.

How it differs from your work: security engineering builds and designs the controls. GRC defines which controls are required, tracks whether they exist, and handles exceptions and audits. GRC people think in policies, frameworks (NIST, ISO), risk registers, and evidence, not in architecture.

Why Ben Evans matters for you: your AI standards (D4/D5) are only useful if GRC can map them to their control framework and audit against them. If they can't, the standards stay on paper.

